  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1021 PM CST Wed Nov 4 2026\n\nTonight through Friday:\n\nSatellite imagery this evening shows the larger weather-making \ntrough to our east continue to shift eastward while lift associated \nwith it joining a shortwave entering the Central Plains from the \nFront Range of the Rockies. The 00z OAX observed sounding features a \nshallow boundary layer with decently steep lapse rates -- to the \npoint where they're contributing to some sprinkle chances and dots \non the windshield for many this evening. Those chance increase as \nthe cloud depth increases, with nighttime RGB imagery showing \nthat the best lift is gradually pivoting southward from SD/MN. \nTemperatures turn chilly tonight as those sprinkles and areas of\ndrizzle move through, with most locations having a 10-20% \nchance at best to see something measurable. Just off the deck, \nwinds are swift with a few gusts of 30+ kts sneaking through the\ninversion already this evening. Those speeds certainly won't be\nreached regularly, but we've given a bit of a nudge upward in \nwind speeds to match through the morning hours tomorrow. \n\nBy sunrise, the low-end chances for a sprinkle or two will still be \nlingering across southeast Nebraska, pointing into Iowa before \nreceding to the ENE. Cloud cover will still linger if forecast \nsoundings are to be believed, making for continued cool and dreary \ndays that end up on the dry side for us. Forecast highs could see a \nbit of a dip compared to what we have in there if the cloud cover is \nthick enough, and overnight lows could stay a bit warmer as well. \nThe next aspect of the forecast to check out would be those \novernight lows Thursday night heading into Friday, specifically the \nlikelihood of reaching freezing or below. Comparing the NBM's \nchances of hitting freezing against the larger EPS/NAEFS members, \nthe influence of the cloud coverage is being undersold and you'll \nhave to travel a bit farther to the northwest to hit freezing (with \nbetter than 50% odds starting along a line from Columbus to Wayne. \nAreas to the southeast will have to wait until Friday night into \nSaturday morning to see a shot at freezing, as widespread lows close \nto 30 degrees area forecast. Double check outdoor hoses and plants \nto minimize any damage, we're pretty behind in terms of the cold, \nI'd bet there are plenty of sprinklers/watering hoses that still \nneed to be put away/winterized.\n\nSaturday and Beyond:\n\nAfter a chilly morning, the busier mid/upper pattern that has been \noverhead will begin turning a bit warmer as mid/upper ridging builds \nin from the northwest. Clearing skies will work with the building \nthermal ridge to the west to help us reach back into the 50s \nSaturday and into the 60s Sunday, making for an excellent weekend to \nenjoy any fall activity that the heart desires. That warming carries \nforward one more day into the upcoming work week, with highs \nreaching near 70 near the KS border, cooling into the middle of next \nweek as the upper pattern returns to zonal flow. Dry conditions may \ncarry us through the remainder of the week as well, with model \nguidance showing little to stir precipitation chances and a nod \ntowards seasonably warm temperatures.",
